+### Profile the CUDA graph capture phase
+
+The tools above profile the steady-state runtime (prefill / decode). To instead profile the **CUDA graph capture phase** that runs once at server startup, launch the server with `--enable-profile-cuda-graph`. This exports one PyTorch Profiler trace per captured batch size, which is useful for diagnosing slow or memory-heavy graph capture.
+
+```bash Command
+# set trace path
+export SGLANG_TORCH_PROFILER_DIR=/root/sglang/profile_log
+
+# launch the server with CUDA graph capture profiling enabled
+python -m sglang.launch_server --model-path meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct --enable-profile-cuda-graph
+```
+
+Behavior and output:
+
+- Traces are written to `$&#123;SGLANG_TORCH_PROFILER_DIR&#125;/capture_traces/` (falls back to `traces/capture_traces/` if the variable is unset).
+- One trace file is produced per captured batch size, named `bs_&#123;bs&#125;_rank0.json.gz`. Per-batch-size trace export is opt-in via `SGLANG_ENABLE_CUDA_GRAPH_CAPTURE_TRACE=1`; without it the profiler still runs for the in-log summary tables and the memory snapshot.
+- The two warmup runs that precede each capture are skipped, so only the capture run is recorded.
+- Only tensor-parallel rank 0 is profiled.
+- A CUDA memory snapshot (`cuda_graph_runner_memory_usage.pickle`) and per-kernel CPU/CUDA time summary tables are also emitted for the capture phase.
+
+The capture traces are viewed the same way as other PyTorch Profiler traces (see [View traces](#view-traces)).
+
+### Kernel Shape Discovery
+
+Many SGLang kernels (Triton attention/MoE kernels, FlashInfer ops, and aiter ops on AMD) are launched in a way that bypasses the PyTorch profiler's operator recording, so they appear in a trace **without** `Input Dims` / `Input type`. This makes it hard to correlate a kernel in the timeline with the tensor shapes it ran on (for example, the `tilelang_sparse_fwd` operation when running GLM-style models shows up with no shape metadata).
+
+**Shape discovery** closes this gap. When enabled, SGLang wraps a registry of kernel entry points (plus auto-discovered kernel launchers under `flashinfer.*`, `sglang.srt.*`, and `aiter.ops.*`) so they are recorded as operator events with their tensor shapes. The implementation lives in `python/sglang/srt/utils/kernel_shape_profiler.py`; it is activated only while a profiling session is running and is fully reverted afterwards, so it has no effect on normal serving.
+
+This feature behaves identically in eager mode and graph (CUDA graph) mode, with one extra opt-in for the graph-capture trace described below.
+
+#### Prerequisites
+
+Shape recording must be enabled for shape discovery to take effect. It is on by default; the relevant environment variables are:
+
+```bash Command
+export SGLANG_TORCH_PROFILER_DIR=/root/sglang/profile_log
+export SGLANG_PROFILE_RECORD_SHAPES=True   # default True; required for shape discovery
+export SGLANG_PROFILE_WITH_STACK=True      # default True; optional, adds call stacks
+```
+
+If `SGLANG_PROFILE_RECORD_SHAPES` is set to `False`, the `shape_discovery` flag is ignored.
+
+#### Execution profiling (eager and graph mode)
+
+Pass `shape_discovery: true` in the `/start_profile` request. The captured trace will then contain shape information for the kernels that were previously missing it.
+
+```bash Command
+# Start profiling with shape discovery enabled
+curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:30000/start_profile \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{
+    "output_dir": "/tmp/profiles",
+    "num_steps": 10,
+    "activities": ["CPU", "GPU"],
+    "shape_discovery": true
+  }'
+```
+
+A typical workflow:
+
+```bash Command
+# Terminal 1: Start the server
+export SGLANG_TORCH_PROFILER_DIR=/tmp/profiles
+export SGLANG_PROFILE_RECORD_SHAPES=True
+python -m sglang.launch_server --model-path meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct
+
+# Terminal 2: Profile a few steps with shape discovery
+curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:30000/start_profile \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"num_steps": 10, "shape_discovery": true}'
+
+# Terminal 3: Generate load
+python -m sglang.bench_serving --backend sglang --num-prompts 100
+```
+
+Open the resulting trace (see [View traces](#view-traces)) and confirm that the previously shape-less kernels now report `Input Dims`.
+
+#### CUDA graph capture profiling
+
+CUDA graph **capture** is profiled separately from execution:
+
+1. In graph mode without any profiling flags, graph capture proceeds without being profiled (unchanged default behavior).
+2. Launch the server with `--enable-profile-cuda-graph` to generate a trace for the graph capture process. By itself, this does **not** run shape discovery.
+3. To additionally record kernel shapes in the graph-capture trace, launch the server with `--enable-shape-discovery-for-cuda-graph-profile` together with `--enable-profile-cuda-graph`:
+
+```bash Command
+python -m sglang.launch_server \
+  --model-path meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \
+  --enable-profile-cuda-graph \
+  --enable-shape-discovery-for-cuda-graph-profile
+```
